{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 35, :comments ["<b>And he shall tell the priest, leimor.</b> The word <b>leimor</b> is superfluous. And our Rabbis of blessed memory in Torat Kohanim interpreted: the priest shall say to him words of restraint — end of quotation. And this is the way of derash, for the verse speaks the words of the one to whom the house belongs. And they of blessed memory interpreted it on the principle of 'if it is not applicable to the words of the house owner, apply it to the words of the priest.' And Ba'al Korban Aharon explained along this path: 'And he shall tell the priest' and the purpose is 'leimor' to him, the priest — that is to say, words of restraint. And according to this, it should have placed the word 'leimor' after 'for a nega seems to me to be in the house'; but surely the Tanna interpreted the word on the principle of 'if it is not applicable.'" "And it is difficult: why did the verse not place the word 'leimor' in the words of the priest, so that the interpretation would follow straightforwardly? And they left us room to explain. And it is according to their own words of blessed memory, which they wrote there in Torat Kohanim, and this is their language: 'Nega' — what does the phrase 'ke-nega' come to teach? Even if he is a learned scholar and knows that it is definitely a nega, he shall not declare it definitively, etc., but rather he shall say 'ke-nega' — end of quotation. And one must know what compelled them of blessed memory to explain thus — for perhaps the reason it said 'ke-nega' and did not say 'nega' is that the verse was careful to say 'nega,' which would lead you to understand that the Torah obligated him to come to the priest and to tell only if he is a learned scholar and knew for certain that it is a nega; but if he is in doubt whether it is a nega or not, he should not come to the priest. Therefore it said 'ke-nega,' that he should come even in case of doubt just as in case of certainty. However, the Tanna's interpretation is that it should have said only 'and he shall tell the priest,' and the matter is self-evident that the telling concerns the nega mentioned immediately after, and therefore he interpreted that the superfluity falls on the specification 'ke-nega' rather than 'nega' — that even if he is a learned scholar, etc., he shall not say 'nega' but 'ke-nega'; and all this is according to the Tanna's view." "And they left us room here as well to investigate their words of blessed memory: for according to what they further interpreted there in Torat Kohanim, and this is its language: 'It seems to me' — and not 'to my lamp,' 'in the house' — to include the upper story, etc., and several interpretations — from now on the verse needs to say 'ke-nega' in order that he say 'it seems to me in the house' to derive those laws, and from where do we know to interpret that even if he knew for certain that it is a nega he shall say 'ke-nega'? And even though it is the way of our Rabbis of blessed memory to interpret verses in this order, nevertheless as long as we can settle the interpretation in a necessary order it is more correct, and in this we have widened our own space — the meaning of the word 'leimor,' that it intends to say along this path: <b>leimor ke-nega</b>, meaning that even though according to truth he knows that it is a nega and it is not 'ke-nega' except in his saying, nevertheless he shall say thus. And the construal of the word 'leimor' is along this path: the saying shall be 'ke-nega,' and from inference you understand that it is not 'ke-nega' except in his saying and not in the actual justice of the matter. And in this we have also accommodated the Tanna's interpretation, which interpreted in the word 'ke-nega' that it means to say that even if he is a learned scholar, etc. It further intends to say that the Torah permits saying 'ke-nega,' and consequently you understand that it is not actually 'ke-nega,' since it was necessary to permit it. And I have already written many times that permission is granted to us to interpret verses in several different ways from the words of our Rabbis of blessed memory, provided the verses settle properly, provided it does not contradict the words of our Rabbis of blessed memory in matters of halakhah."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:57:09.599963683Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}